In `UFGRevenueGovernance`, a guardian nomination cannot be cancelled and never expires. A nominee whose key is compromised, or a nomination that governance no longer wants, stays acceptable until another 7-day proposal replaces it.

#### Technical Details

[`nominateGuardian`](https://github.com/yAudit/ufg-yagent-scan/blob/main/UFG-revenue/src/UFGRevenueGovernance.sol#L99-L104) can only be called by the timelock, and it rejects `address(0)`:

```solidity
function nominateGuardian(address next) external {
    if (msg.sender != address(TIMELOCK)) revert Unauthorized();
    if (next == address(0) || next == guardian) revert BadAddress();
    pendingGuardian = next;
    emit GuardianNominated(next);
}
```

[`acceptGuardian`](https://github.com/yAudit/ufg-yagent-scan/blob/main/UFG-revenue/src/UFGRevenueGovernance.sol#L106-L111) has no deadline, and no function clears `pendingGuardian`. Controller nominations have `cancelControllerNomination`; guardian nominations have no equivalent.

To revoke a nomination, governance has to schedule another `nominateGuardian` with an address nobody can accept (for example `0x…dEaD`), which takes the full 7-day delay. Until that executes, the nominee can call `acceptGuardian` at any time. A nomination left in place is also still acceptable years later.

#### Impact

Low. The guardian cannot move assets, but known issue K3 says it "can block all maintenance, including its own replacement": it can cancel every proposal and every controller nomination. If an unwanted nominee accepts, either because its key was compromised during the 7-day window or because a stale nomination was forgotten, it can never be removed. Governance is then frozen: no splitter upgrade, converter, route, destination or keeper change can execute. The preconditions (a compromised or unwanted nominee, before governance can replace the nomination) keep the likelihood low.

#### Recommendation

Let the controller or the current guardian cancel a pending guardian nomination, and give the nomination an expiry:

```diff
+ uint256 public guardianNominationExpiresAt;
+
  function nominateGuardian(address next) external {
      if (msg.sender != address(TIMELOCK)) revert Unauthorized();
      if (next == address(0) || next == guardian) revert BadAddress();
      pendingGuardian = next;
+     guardianNominationExpiresAt = block.timestamp + DELAY;
      emit GuardianNominated(next);
  }
+
+ function cancelGuardianNomination() external {
+     if (msg.sender != controller && msg.sender != guardian) revert Unauthorized();
+     pendingGuardian = address(0);
+     emit GuardianNominated(address(0));
+ }

  function acceptGuardian() external {
-     if (msg.sender != pendingGuardian) revert Unauthorized();
+     if (msg.sender != pendingGuardian || block.timestamp > guardianNominationExpiresAt) revert Unauthorized();
```

#### Developer Response

Accepted.

Fixed in ufgfinancialdefi/ufg-audit@cd3abce (tag `audit-2026-10-fixes`). A guardian nomination carries a 7-day acceptance window in `guardianReadyUntil`. After that window `acceptGuardian` reverts with `NotReady`. The controller or the current guardian can call `cancelGuardianNomination`, as with controller nominations. The roles table and the guardian change paragraph of the brief are updated.

The live Pons escrow reverts when a recipient with no credit claims. The router calls it on every `collect()` and `redeem()`, catches the revert and logs it as `FeeClaimFailed`. Between Pons sweeps every claim is empty, so holders pay about 7,100 gas for a failing call on every redeem.

#### Technical Details

`_claimFromPons` always calls the escrow and logs any revert as a failure ([`UFGLaunchFeeRouter.sol#L168-L176`](https://github.com/ufgfinancialdefi/ufg-audit/blob/50824c3e32eaa9a2ebc0d1d1e9c0895522e73aae/UFG-factory/src/UFGLaunchFeeRouter.sol#L168-L176)):

```solidity
try PONS_ESCROW.claim() returns (uint256) {} catch (bytes memory reason) { emit FeeClaimFailed(reason); }
```

The live [`PonsV2FeeEscrow`](https://repo.sourcify.dev/4663/0xd3AFEB2a57f70eF218Aa82451c51B2fb0416Ac9e) reverts with `NoBalance()` when the caller has no credit (`_claim` at L108 and `_claimToken` at L124: `if (amount == 0) revert NoBalance();`). Every `collect()` and every `redeem()` that runs while the router has no new credit therefore emits `FeeClaimFailed(0xc2caa2a6)`. On a coin that trades rarely, that is most calls.

The event only records the caught revert, with its reason. The cost is gas: the escrow call fails on every `collect()` and every `redeem()` between Pons sweeps, and on a `redeem()` the holder pays for it.

#### Impact

Informational. No funds are at risk. Each empty claim costs a failing external call, which holders pay on every `redeem()` between sweeps. Measured on a fork of Robinhood Chain against the live escrow, the check in the recommendation lowers `collect()` with nothing credited from 58,090 to 50,955 gas (7,135 saved, about 12%) and removes the event. `redeem()` runs the same claim first. With credit waiting the check adds 1,047 gas (130,911 to 131,958), because the extra view call comes before the claim. Between Pons sweeps every claim is empty, so the check saves gas overall.

#### Recommendation

Skip the escrow call when there is nothing to claim. Read the credit inside `try` too, so that an escrow outage still lets `claim()` deliver the funds already held, as it does now:

```diff
     function _claimFromPons() private returns (uint256 claimed) {
+        if (quote == address(0)) {
+            try PONS_ESCROW.balanceOf(address(this)) returns (uint256 credit) { if (credit == 0) return 0; } catch {}
+        } else {
+            try PONS_ESCROW.balanceOfToken(address(this), quote) returns (uint256 credit) { if (credit == 0) return 0; } catch {}
+        }
         uint256 before = _held();
```

#### Developer Response

Accepted. One correction for the record: the brief does not present `FeeClaimFailed` as a signal. The event records a caught revert. However the point about gas is correct, and holders pay it on every redeem between sweeps which is wasteful.

Fixed in ufgfinancialdefi/ufg-audit@af05aa3 (tag `audit-2026-10-fixes`). `_claimFromPons` reads the router's escrow credit first and returns when it is zero. Both calls run inside `try`, so an escrow outage still lets `claim()` deliver what the router holds. The mock escrow reverts with `NoBalance()` on an empty claim, as the live one does. Tests cover the empty claim, a logged failure, and a reverting balance view.
